  "hints": [
    "Backtracking is the right frame, but the board can have an enormous number of completions, so the counter must saturate: stop the whole search the moment a second completion is found.",
    "A digit is legal at a cell when the row, the column and the 3x3 box all lack it. Keep one 9-bit mask per unit so that check is a single AND instead of a scan of 27 cells.",
    "Branch on the empty cell that has the fewest legal digits, not on the first empty cell in reading order, and prune immediately when some empty cell has no legal digit at all."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force first validates the pre-filled cells against each other, then scans the board in row-major order, and at every empty cell tries digits 1 to 9, accepting a digit by comparing it against the 9 cells of its row, the 9 cells of its column and the 9 cells of its box (up to 27 comparisons per attempt). Reaching the last cell with a legal placement increments a counter, and both the counter and every recursive frame abandon work as soon as the counter reaches two. The optimal replaces those scans with three arrays of nine bitmasks, one mask per row, column and box, where bit d is set exactly when digit d is already used in that unit. A candidate test becomes free = ~(rows[r] | cols[c] | boxes[b]) restricted to bits 1..9, and placing or removing a digit is an OR followed by an AND-NOT on three masks. On top of that it always branches on the empty cell with the fewest legal digits (minimum remaining values), and returns immediately when a cell has none. Both versions read the board through the same normalisation pass, which pads a short or ragged board with empty cells and rejects any cell whose value is not an integer in 0..9, so an illegal board returns 0 without ever entering the search.",
    "why_optimal": "Sudoku completion is exactly-cover style search, and no polynomial algorithm is known, so both programs are exponential in the number of empty cells e and the correct claim is about constants and tree shape rather than about asymptotics. The mask version answers 'is this digit legal here' with one bitwise AND instead of a scan of up to 27 cells, and minimum-remaining-values ordering usually reduces the branching factor from 9 to 1 or 2 at most nodes while detecting dead branches one level earlier. At most two solutions are ever enumerated, so the work is bounded by the time to find the second completion, which is what keeps an empty board fast for both versions.",
    "pitfalls": "Pre-filled digits must be checked against each other before searching: two 3s in one row make the board invalid and the answer 0 even though no empty cell is ever filled, and a search that only validates newly placed digits reports 1 for such a board. The candidate mask must keep bit 0 clear, normally with (1 << 10) - 2, otherwise digit 0 gets placed on empty cells. Digits run 1..9, so iterating 0..8 (or comparing a cell against 0 as if it were a value) shifts every digit and breaks the box test. Removing a digit must clear exactly that bit from all three masks, and the early exit must be checked before recursing; clearing the wrong mask makes later branches see phantom conflicts, and a missing counter check lets the search enumerate millions of completions. A fully filled legal board must return 1 rather than falling through the empty-cell loop and returning 0. Finally, every cell is validated before the search starts: a value outside 0 through 9 makes the board illegal and the answer 0. Without that check an out-of-range value reaches the shift 1 << v, which raises in Python and silently wraps modulo 32 in JavaScript, so the two languages would disagree instead of both rejecting the board."
  },
